{"title":"2026 BMT Individual Problems/Problem 20","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_20\">Problem 20</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_BMT_Individual_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 20\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/6521ad84932f05bbfa1185b24d793813c7aefc2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C, D,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"85\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lie on a circle in that order such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/f/2bfaa86e188b815f259ec79ec03d68d9808387a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABD = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"109\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/6/e06b52410f4d0f0c89d0e08a5ba39c4cdb6dcdbc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADC = \\angle CBE = 60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"189\" height=\"15\" />. Segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> intersect at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. The circle passing through points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/f/ccf6d387ce491c0ca100c134021f21f616d75162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> intersects <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/c/4ec42d731653ed6bea8a4c5f8cdd4a1de45a8bc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G \\ne A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" />. Given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/5/855f3b2a26f0b3cdee3caac7796c109dd88a83a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BF = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/0/c102879f85158a055d59dfeb580d085ead9292f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DF = 8$\" width=\"62\" height=\"12\" />, compute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b11fca7fa0396390456782405b2897976a413f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Coordinate_Geometry.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Coordinate_Geometry)\">Solution 1 (Coordinate Geometry)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_BMT_Individual_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Coordinate Geometry)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/f/2bfaa86e188b815f259ec79ec03d68d9808387a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABD = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"109\" height=\"15\" />, segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> is a diameter of the circle. We can deduce the angle measures of the arcs from the given information:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/b/03b6c980833711545ef47dcbe863304ddd0866cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADC = 60^\\circ$\" width=\"104\" height=\"13\" />, arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/6/2d6fa6261a67d78ece1427d996638b426760d55b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\widehat{ABC} = 120^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"104\" height=\"21\" />, which leaves arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/7/db7f0de4d9d9c54ea24c5250a277a84df173965f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\widehat{CD} = 180^\\circ - 120^\\circ = 60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"198\" height=\"22\" />.</li>\n<li>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/4/6041188129ab4850df446da44aa29b574c04a8e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CBE = 60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"15\" />, arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/3/e63f408a241d7856473756f8b63195ce60ece6f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\widehat{CE} = 120^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" />. Subtracting arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/6/596c7f550d68cf901019578af9835a2d90b679cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\widehat{CD}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"20\" /> tells us that arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53378d5b05ec5f66528b26f3919bd22633ae574.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\widehat{DE} = 60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"82\" height=\"21\" />.</li></ul>\n<p>Let's set up a convenient Cartesian coordinate system. Place the center of the main circle at the origin <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Orienting the diameter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/5/bc56862b1fdb71204894da278e42ef75de73a40b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> along the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-axis gives us the endpoints <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/a/e4af044c59fd7dfa28085ca2991e83f923545507.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(R, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/f/acf57d65592e3e6a1d6a9446c25b772d07f5f848.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(-R, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"20\" />. Using our arc measurements, we can find the exact coordinates of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/8/ea82dde4c392e96d070b64366a9495931e6b2c0b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[C\\left(\\frac{R}{2}, \\frac{R\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad E\\left(\\frac{R}{2}, -\\frac{R\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\]\" width=\"300\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Notice that the line segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" /> lies entirely on the vertical line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e782e0f5fb6d8896d3d9deb4d68159a39f875486.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{R}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"54\" height=\"39\" />. Since point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the intersection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/c/13c970cbf5e2e7029089517d5e8efbb8cade6d8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, its <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-coordinate must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/0/df0fecfed9322b5023852f3b182fb88eca9ec28f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_F = \\frac{R}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"65\" height=\"39\" />.\n</p><p>We are given that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/5/855f3b2a26f0b3cdee3caac7796c109dd88a83a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BF = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/0/c102879f85158a055d59dfeb580d085ead9292f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DF = 8$\" width=\"62\" height=\"12\" />, which means the total length of chord <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/7/5e7a1b6098a8c98b8e4adae526aeef4b91712620.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> divides the segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> in a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/0/ad0a4bc0ad5d6415575678e779682fb207069c5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5:4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"35\" height=\"14\" /> ratio. Applying the section formula to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-coordinates yields:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/4/3741ed845586e4e998044f0e1710e41baab8c33d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_F = \\frac{4x_B + 5x_D}{9} \\implies \\frac{R}{2} = \\frac{4x_B + 5R}{9} \\implies x_B = -\\frac{R}{8}\\]\" width=\"431\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Because point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> lies on the circle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/a/b9aa644c5501de59f7ec14217a7eafb92e2a7048.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + y^2 = R^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" />, its <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />-coordinate is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/c/1dc96bea6e1fc78fc5e10143b2f388e9d1b7e082.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y_B = \\sqrt{R^2 - \\left(-\\frac{R}{8}\\right)^2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}R\\]\" width=\"246\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Now, we can find the radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> by writing out the distance formula for chord <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/a/1cac1073873687880aad688d24089bbd93f590b3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[BD = \\sqrt{\\left(-\\frac{R}{8} - R\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}R\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{81R^2}{64} + \\frac{63R^2}{64}} = \\frac{12R}{8} = \\frac{3}{2}R\\]\" width=\"546\" height=\"63\" />\n</p><p>Setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a651f8b239eed7a7dd84da82dd8c7bcc215b60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{2}R = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"71\" height=\"38\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/e/bfed9a574d1f8454da7300f4f5548a89b902d26c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"58\" height=\"14\" />. Substituting this back into our expressions gives the exact positions of our key points:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/7/a3774e0fecdb5ef7902627c21234a9bd774af89e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A(-12, 0), \\quad E(6, -6\\sqrt{3}), \\quad \\text{and} \\quad F(6, 2\\sqrt{7})\\]\" width=\"340\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Next, let's look at the second circle passing through points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/f/ccf6d387ce491c0ca100c134021f21f616d75162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/8/ce88d73e0a92ad31a26f6f7f9ed07e4f4e8bf634.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABF = \\angle ABD = 90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"188\" height=\"15\" />, segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/d/c9daba06f5ec9c5069f24bbeab9c7151ab4f68c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> serves as the diameter of this new circle. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies on this circle, the inscribed angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/f/5cf58ba244b2e987eccdcab2453bca99cc8d4643.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AGF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/e/22ec0d2e4202839322d26a23dcb13f79821df5d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$90^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" />. This tells us that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is simply the foot of the perpendicular dropped from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />, meaning <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b11fca7fa0396390456782405b2897976a413f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is the geometric projection of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/d/c9daba06f5ec9c5069f24bbeab9c7151ab4f68c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> onto <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/6/9a65c05bd85d2609bea5db2b109ed5f556463511.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AE$\" width=\"27\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>The line passing through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/a/aaa333e4fee3c3eb798773b3aa9ed41bd8f799bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(-12,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bb1e18a808de858197298910c85c5be5b8714af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E(6, -6\\sqrt{3})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"95\" height=\"24\" /> has a slope of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/5/fd5d2db82e4085479ce563c33d5a222d10cfe6c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"44\" height=\"43\" />, which gives it the linear equation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/4/a843b1ef642772d7848e66e006071accf0f5cec9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x + \\sqrt{3}y + 12 = 0\\]\" width=\"139\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>The perpendicular line passing through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/5/2b5ae9815d89b6a9abed15df43f56dc39c2010eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F(6, 2\\sqrt{7})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"81\" height=\"24\" /> must have a slope of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/7/6c77cf4b63af80bfbc6a9ad9b4b9e8e549e77d78.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />, meaning its equation is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/2/9224e8174e820063e70021a6f91fab13b92b5553.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[y - 2\\sqrt{7} = \\sqrt{3}(x - 6)\\]\" width=\"167\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Solving this system of equations for the intersection point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> gives its <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-coordinate:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/2/d325176eb20ef99cab82dca76454a216ddcab0e9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_G = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}\\]\" width=\"112\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>To find the distance <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b11fca7fa0396390456782405b2897976a413f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, we calculate its horizontal shift from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/3/a13e9881f49cc030155e005ba729701edc60d032.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x_G - x_A = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{21}}{2} + 12 = \\frac{27 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}\\]\" width=\"298\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> lies on the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/4/24442884d779d434c202e4bc40ee560c9a36afdf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + \\sqrt{3}y + 12 = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"142\" height=\"23\" />, its vertical distance satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/b/9bb7639b2917419ffa4b4674496fb294cacbcce6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y_G^2 = \\frac{(x_G + 12)^2}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"134\" height=\"41\" />. Plugging this into the standard distance formula from point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/a/aaa333e4fee3c3eb798773b3aa9ed41bd8f799bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A(-12,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"79\" height=\"20\" /> allows us to solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/1/b11fca7fa0396390456782405b2897976a413f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AG$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> directly without dealing with messy fractions:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/6/766a9c4d35dc0a73a64c1f707601806e1993198b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AG^2 = (x_G + 12)^2 + y_G^2 = (x_G + 12)^2 + \\frac{(x_G + 12)^2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}(x_G + 12)^2\\]\" width=\"521\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/3/ce3986caad05325b033defe6df2bc5bb2c3b8ef9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AG = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}(x_G + 12) = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\left(\\frac{27 - \\sqrt{21}}{2}\\right) = \\frac{27 - \\sqrt{21}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\boxed{9\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{7}}\\]\" width=\"535\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p><br />\n~Icosahedron0288\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_BMT_Individual_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_BMT_Individual_Problems\" title=\"2026 BMT Individual Problems\">2026 BMT Individual Problems</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Berkeley_Math_Tournament\" title=\"Berkeley Math Tournament\">Berkeley Math Tournament</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017132100\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.032 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.087 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 463/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097